# Thematic Coding — Open-Ended Responses

This document records the coding scheme applied to the six open-ended questionnaire
items (Q11, Q13, Q14, Q16, Q17, Q18; see `docs/questionnaire.pdf`) during the
qualitative analysis in Chapter 3 of the dissertation. Coding followed Braun & Clarke's
thematic analysis process: familiarization, initial coding, theme development, and
review, applied manually to the 32 free-text responses in
`data/processed/survey_responses_clean.csv`.

Three principal themes were identified, consistent with Section 3.3 of the dissertation.

---

## Theme A — Speed vs. Contextual Fidelity
**Source items:** Q11 (preference + why), Q13 (speed), Q14 (context/idioms)

| Code | Description | Approx. respondents citing it |
|---|---|---|
| `speed_ai` | AI praised chiefly for speed/convenience, often in short, low-elaboration justifications | ~12 |
| `context_human` | Human translation praised for handling context, emotion, or cultural nuance | ~14 |
| `literal_ai_weakness` | AI criticized for word-for-word / literal handling of idioms or figurative language | ~8 |
| `task_dependent` | Preference framed as conditional on text type (e.g., "poetic vs. scientific") | ~3 |

**Pattern:** Respondents preferring AI justified this almost entirely in terms of speed
and accessibility, with minimal reference to translation quality. Respondents preferring
human translation gave longer, more elaborated justifications built around context,
emotion, and cultural understanding, and frequently acknowledged AI's speed advantage
even while rejecting it as a full substitute — i.e., praise and critique co-occurred within
the same response rather than defining opposed camps.

---

## Theme B — Premium Cost vs. Cost-Free Efficiency
**Source item:** Q16 (willingness to pay for human translation + why)

| Code | Description |
|---|---|
| `wtp_no_sufficient` | Declines to pay: AI is "good enough" / free / already meets their needs |
| `wtp_yes_accuracy` | Willing to pay: human translation is more accurate / natural / trustworthy |
| `wtp_yes_stakes` | Willing to pay: conditional on high-stakes context (official documents, conferences, diplomas) |
| `wtp_yes_scarcity` | Willing to pay: anticipates human translation becoming a scarce/premium skill as AI expands |
| `wtp_ambivalent` | "Maybe" / "if necessary" — conditional, non-committal |

**Pattern:** Willingness to pay clusters around perceived stakes — respondents were far
more willing to pay for human translation for official, professional, or emotionally
significant content than for everyday use, where free AI tools were seen as sufficient.

---

## Theme C — Structural Recommendations for AI Translation Software
**Source item:** Q17 (suggested improvements)

| Code | Description | Approx. respondents citing it |
|---|---|---|
| `whole_text_not_word` | Wants translation of overall meaning, not word-by-word decoding | ~5 |
| `context_awareness` | Wants improved context/idiom/cultural-nuance handling | ~7 |
| `emotional_tone` | Wants recognition of emotional tone / less "robotic" output | ~3 |
| `dialect_language_coverage` | Wants broader language/dialect coverage | ~2 |
| `satisfied_no_change` | Considers current AI translation sufficient ("flawless") | 1 |

**Pattern:** The dominant, recurring diagnosis is that AI translation processes text at
the word or phrase level rather than the level of overall meaning — this single critique
underlies most of the concrete suggestions offered (context awareness, idiom handling,
tone, cultural nuance).

---

## Notes on method

- Coding was performed manually on the full free-text corpus (32 responses × 6 open
  items = up to 192 text cells, many blank/non-substantive).
- No formal inter-rater reliability statistic was computed (single-coder analysis), which
  is noted as a limitation in Chapter 3.5 of the dissertation and should be disclosed if
  this coding scheme is reused or extended.
- This file documents the *coding categories*, not verbatim transcriptions of every
  response; see `data/processed/survey_responses_clean.csv` for the full original
  free-text answers (already anonymized — no names or identifying data were ever
  collected in the Google Form).
